<section id="Pt2" class="ltx_part">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_part">Appendix</h2>

<section id="A1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix A </span>Additional Related Work</h3>

<section id="A1.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.1 </span>Empirical Findings on Scaling Laws</h4>

<div id="A1.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Scaling laws have emerged as a foundational principle in understanding the interplay between model size, dataset size, and performance in large-scale deep model pre-training. Initially proposed by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>]</cite>, these laws have been further refined through a series of subsequent studies <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>, <a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>, <a href="#bib.bib70" title="" class="ltx_ref">70</a>, <a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>, <a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>, <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>, <a href="#bib.bib57" title="" class="ltx_ref">57</a>, <a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>, <a href="#bib.bib60" title="" class="ltx_ref">60</a>, <a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">42</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Among these, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>]</cite> introduced the Chinchilla Law, which approximates the final-step risk <math id="A1.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}</annotation></semantics></math> as follows:</p>
<table id="A1.Ex18" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}=R_{0}+A_{1}D^{-\kappa_{1}}+A_{2}M^{-\kappa_{2}}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>D</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>M</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}=R_{0}+A_{1}D^{-\kappa_{1}}+A_{2}M^{-\kappa_{2}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A1.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A1.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A1.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A1.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="A1.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> are constants, and <math id="A1.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.SS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> represent the amount of training data (tokens) and model size (number of parameters), respectively. Based on this risk prediction, they formulate the following compute-optimal scaling problem:</p>
<table id="A1.Ex19" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatornamewithlimits{min}_{DM=C}R_{0}+A_{1}D^{-\kappa_{1}}+A_{2}M^{-\kappa_{2}}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mo>min</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>D</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>M</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatornamewithlimits{min}_{DM=C}R_{0}+A_{1}D^{-\kappa_{1}}+A_{2}M^{-\kappa_{2}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS1.p2.3" class="ltx_p">where the compute budget <math id="A1.SS1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C=DM" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C=DM</annotation></semantics></math> serves as the scaling bottleneck.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Furthermore, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib60" title="" class="ltx_ref">60</a>]</cite> and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">42</a>]</cite> empirically investigated scaling laws in the context of LRSs. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib60" title="" class="ltx_ref">60</a>]</cite> heuristically proposed the Momentum Law to characterize the impact of LRSs on scaling laws, where the population risk at the <math id="A1.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-th step follows:</p>
<table id="A1.Ex20" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}_{k}=R_{0}+AS_{1}^{-\kappa}-CS_{2}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>κ</mi></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}_{k}=R_{0}+AS_{1}^{-\kappa}-CS_{2},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS1.p3.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A1.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A1.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A1.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="A1.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>κ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa</annotation></semantics></math> are constant parameters, and <math id="A1.SS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.SS1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> roughly denote the area under the learning rate curve and the learning rate annealing area, respectively, which are formally defined as follows:</p>
<table id="A1.Ex21" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{1}:=\sum_{i=1}^{k}\eta_{i},\quad\quad S_{2}:=\sum_{i=2}^{k}\sum_{j=2}^{i}(\eta_{j-1}-\eta_{j})\lambda^{i-j}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">:=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></munderover><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">:=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></munderover><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mi>i</mi></munderover><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{1}:=\sum_{i=1}^{k}\eta_{i},\quad\quad S_{2}:=\sum_{i=2}^{k}\sum_{j=2}^{i}(\eta_{j-1}-\eta_{j})\lambda^{i-j},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS1.p3.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A1.SS1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\eta}:=(\eta_{1},\eta_{2},\cdots,\eta_{K})^{\top}\in\mathbb{R}_{+}^{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝜼</mi><mo>:=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><msubsup><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>K</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\eta}:=(\eta_{1},\eta_{2},\cdots,\eta_{K})^{\top}\in\mathbb{R}_{+}^{K}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the learning rate schedule and <math id="A1.SS1.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> is a hyper-parameter representing the decay factor for LR annealing momentum, which typically ranges from <math id="A1.SS1.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.99" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.99</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.99</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="A1.SS1.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.999" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.999</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.999</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Subsequently, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">42</a>]</cite> further developed an empirical law, termed the Multi-Power Law, in which they replaced <math id="A1.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> in the Momentum Law with additional power laws to account for the loss reduction effect as the learning rate decays. The Multi-Power Law says that the risk can be predicted as:</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<table id="A1.Ex22" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A1.Ex22X"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.Ex22X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{R}_{k}=R_{0}+AS_{1}^{-\kappa}-\operatorname{LD}(k)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>κ</mi></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>LD</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{R}_{k}=R_{0}+AS_{1}^{-\kappa}-\operatorname{LD}(k),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A1.Ex22Xa"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.Ex22Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle LD(k):=C\sum_{i=2}^{k}(\eta_{i-1}-\eta_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle LD(k):=C\sum_{i=2}^{k}(\eta_{i-1}-\eta_{i})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.Ex22Xa.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle G(\eta_{i}^{-\kappa^{\prime}}S_{i}),\quad\quad S_{i}:=\sum_{j=1}^{i}\eta_{j},\quad\quad G(x):=1-(C^{\prime}x+1)^{-\kappa^{\prime\prime}}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>η</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>κ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>i</mi></munderover></mstyle><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>κ</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle G(\eta_{i}^{-\kappa^{\prime}}S_{i}),\quad\quad S_{i}:=\sum_{j=1}^{i}\eta_{j},\quad\quad G(x):=1-(C^{\prime}x+1)^{-\kappa^{\prime\prime}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A1.SS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{0},A,C,C^{\prime},\kappa,\kappa^{\prime},\kappa^{\prime\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>C</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>κ</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>κ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>κ</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{0},A,C,C^{\prime},\kappa,\kappa^{\prime},\kappa^{\prime\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> are constants.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">However, both the Momentum Law and the Multi-Power Law remain empirical, lacking a rigorous theoretical foundation. In our work, we build on a solvable model and theoretically derive a novel functional-form scaling law with arbitrary LRS, which also generalizes well for unseen larger model sizes.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.2 </span>SGD for Kernel Regression</h4>

<div id="A1.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Several works have analyzed the SGD algorithm within the context of high-dimensional kernel regression <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>, <a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">38</a>, <a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">53</a>, <a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">47</a>, <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>, <a href="#bib.bib62" title="" class="ltx_ref">62</a>]</cite>. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite> demonstrated that averaged-iteration SGD with a constant learning rate can achieve statistical optimality in the easy-task regime. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">38</a>]</cite> showed that multiple-pass, non-averaged SGD also attains statistical optimality in this regime, while <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">53</a>]</cite> established that multiple-pass, averaged-iteration SGD can achieve optimality in the hard-task regime. Building on these results, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">47</a>]</cite> analyzed the convergence behavior of SGD in high-dimensional kernel regression, demonstrating that strategies such as averaging and multiple passes can enhance learning performance. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite> and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib62" title="" class="ltx_ref">62</a>]</cite> studied the noiseless case: <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite> analyzed SGD for a noiseless linear model, deriving convergence results and extending the findings to online function estimation. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib62" title="" class="ltx_ref">62</a>]</cite> examined SGD in a noiseless least-squares setup, showing convergence for non-strongly convex problems and faster-than-usual convergence rates in over-parameterized settings.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A2" class="ltx_appendix">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix B </span>Experiment Details and Additional Results</h3>

<div id="A2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we present the details of our experiments as well as additional results.</p>
</div>
<section id="A2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.1 </span>Popular Learning Rate Schedules</h4>

<div id="A2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Here, we introduce some widely used LRSs in the context of LLM pre-training.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="A2.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A2.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A2.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Cosine Schedule <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">41</a>]</cite>.</span> The schedule is given by <math id="A2.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta_{k}=\frac{1+\rho}{2}\eta_{\operatorname{max}}+\frac{1-\rho}{2}\eta_{\operatorname{max}}\operatorname{cos}(\frac{k-1}{K-1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>ρ</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>max</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>ρ</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta_{k}=\frac{1+\rho}{2}\eta_{\operatorname{max}}+\frac{1-\rho}{2}\eta_{\operatorname{max}}\operatorname{cos}(\frac{k-1}{K-1})</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="A2.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta_{\operatorname{max}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta_{\operatorname{max}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the maximum learning rate and the hyper-parameter <math id="A2.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math> is usually chosen as <math id="A2.I1.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.1</annotation></semantics></math> such that the minimum learning rate is <math id="A2.I1.i1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta_{\operatorname{max}}/10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta_{\operatorname{max}}/10</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib61" title="" class="ltx_ref">61</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A2.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A2.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Warmup-Stable-Decay (WSD) Schedule <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib70" title="" class="ltx_ref">70</a>, <a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>, <a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite>.</span> The schedule consists of three phases: a warm-up phase of <math id="A2.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{\operatorname{warm-up}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>warm</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>up</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{\operatorname{warm-up}}</annotation></semantics></math> steps, followed by a stable phase maintaining the learning rate <math id="A2.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta_{k}=\eta_{\operatorname{max}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>max</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta_{k}=\eta_{\operatorname{max}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and finally a decay phase governed by <math id="A2.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta_{k}=h(k-K_{\operatorname{stable}})\eta_{\operatorname{max}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>stable</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>max</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta_{k}=h(k-K_{\operatorname{stable}})\eta_{\operatorname{max}}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="A2.I1.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{\operatorname{stable}}\leqslant k\leqslant K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>stable</mi></msub><mo>⩽</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>⩽</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{\operatorname{stable}}\leqslant k\leqslant K</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="A2.I1.i2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{\operatorname{stable}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>stable</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{\operatorname{stable}}</annotation></semantics></math> represents the total duration of the first two phases. Here, the decay function <math id="A2.I1.i2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h(\cdot)\in(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h(\cdot)\in(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math> can be linear or exponential.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A2.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A2.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Multi-Step Schedule <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite>.</span> The entire schedule is divided into <math id="A2.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> stages, i.e., <math id="A2.I1.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[K_{0},K_{1}]\cup[K_{1},K_{2}]\cup\cdots\cup[K_{S-1},K_{S}]=[0,K]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∪</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>S</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[K_{0},K_{1}]\cup[K_{1},K_{2}]\cup\cdots\cup[K_{S-1},K_{S}]=[0,K]</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="A2.I1.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0=K_{0}&lt;K_{1}&lt;\cdots&lt;K_{S}=K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.1389em">&lt;</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.1389em">⋯</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em">&lt;</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0=K_{0}&lt;K_{1}&lt;\cdots&lt;K_{S}=K</annotation></semantics></math>. The schedule satisfies that <math id="A2.I1.i3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta_{k}=\eta_{K_{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta_{k}=\eta_{K_{i}}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="A2.I1.i3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{i-1}&lt;k\leqslant K_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>⩽</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{i-1}&lt;k\leqslant K_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> (<math id="A2.I1.i3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leqslant i\leqslant S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>⩽</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>⩽</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leqslant i\leqslant S</annotation></semantics></math>). In our LLM experiments, we consider a 8-1-1 LRS, corresponding to the case where <math id="A2.I1.i3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S=3</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="A2.I1.i3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta_{K_{1}}=\eta_{\max},\eta_{K_{2}}=\eta_{\max}/\sqrt{10}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><msub><mi>K</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>max</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><msub><mi>K</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msqrt><mn>10</mn></msqrt></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta_{K_{1}}=\eta_{\max},\eta_{K_{2}}=\eta_{\max}/\sqrt{10}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="A2.I1.i3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta_{K_{3}}=\eta_{\max}/10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><msub><mi>K</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta_{K_{3}}=\eta_{\max}/10</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="A2.I1.i3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{1}=0.8K,K_{2}=0.9K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>0.8</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>0.9</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{1}=0.8K,K_{2}=0.9K</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.2 </span>Teacher-Student Kernel Regression</h4>

<section id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Physical-time FSL.</h6>

<div id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The FSL (<a href="#S4.E7" title="In Theorem 4.1 (Intrinsic-time Functional Scaling Law (FSL)). ‣ 4 Functional Scaling Laws ‣ Unveiling the Role of Learning Rate Schedules via Functional Scaling Laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>) is presented in terms of intrinsic time, but in practice, it is often more convenient to use physical time (training steps). By a suitable change of times, after <math id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> steps (equivalently, <math id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau/h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau/h</annotation></semantics></math> discrete steps), the FSL maintains the form (<a href="#S4.E7" title="In Theorem 4.1 (Intrinsic-time Functional Scaling Law (FSL)). ‣ 4 Functional Scaling Laws ‣ Unveiling the Role of Learning Rate Schedules via Functional Scaling Laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>), with adjustments:</p>
<table id="A2.EGx3" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A2.Ex23"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex23.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle t^{-s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle t^{-s}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex23.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=T(\tau)^{-s}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=T(\tau)^{-s},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A2.Ex24"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex24.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{N}(\varphi,b)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>φ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{N}(\varphi,b)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex24.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\int_{0}^{\tau}\mathcal{K}(T(\tau)-T(u))\,(e(T(u))+\sigma^{2})\frac{h\varphi(u)^{2}}{b(u)}\mathop{}\!\mathrm{d}u." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mn>0</mn><mi>τ</mi></msubsup></mstyle><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>φ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\int_{0}^{\tau}\mathcal{K}(T(\tau)-T(u))\,(e(T(u))+\sigma^{2})\frac{h\varphi(u)^{2}}{b(u)}\mathop{}\!\mathrm{d}u.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Fitting FSL on SGD Average-Risks.</h6>

<div id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To validate that the Functional Scaling law (FSL) can accurately
capture the risk curve of SGD, we conducted a
series of SGD experiments under different configurations of <math id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math>.
Subsequently, we fitted the FSL to these risk curves.
Our results demonstrate that FSL indeed provides a close fit to the SGD
trajectories.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In each experiment, we adopt a teacher-student model configuration with
<math id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M=N=128" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>128</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M=N=128</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma=3</annotation></semantics></math> and employ the top-<math id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> projection matrix, thereby eliminating the
approximation error term <math id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M^{-s\beta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>M</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M^{-s\beta}</annotation></semantics></math>. We explore a range of values for
<math id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s\in[0.5,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0.5</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s\in[0.5,1]</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta\in[1.5,5]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>1.5</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta\in[1.5,5]</annotation></semantics></math>, encompassing both easy- (<math id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s\geqslant 1-1/\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>⩾</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s\geqslant 1-1/\beta</annotation></semantics></math>) and
hard-learning (<math id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s&lt;1-1/\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s&lt;1-1/\beta</annotation></semantics></math>) regimes. For each parameter configuration, we execute 200
independent SGD runs with a batch size of 1 over 10,000 steps. The resulting
average trajectory across these runs serves as the fitting target. The FSL
fitting is performed using the physical-time FSL formulation.</p>
<table id="A2.Ex25" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex25.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}_{k}=c_{1}T(k)^{-s}+c_{2}\int_{0}^{k}\mathcal{K}(T(k)-T(u))e(T(u))\varphi(u)^{2}\mathop{}\!\mathrm{d}u+c_{3}\sigma^{2}\int_{0}^{k}\mathcal{K}(T(k)-T(u))\varphi(u)^{2}\mathop{}\!\mathrm{d}u," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mn>0</mn><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>φ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mn>0</mn><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>φ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}_{k}=c_{1}T(k)^{-s}+c_{2}\int_{0}^{k}\mathcal{K}(T(k)-T(u))e(T(u))\varphi(u)^{2}\mathop{}\!\mathrm{d}u+c_{3}\sigma^{2}\int_{0}^{k}\mathcal{K}(T(k)-T(u))\varphi(u)^{2}\mathop{}\!\mathrm{d}u,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{3}</annotation></semantics></math> are constants to fit.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Fitting the SGD trajectory, we minimize the mean squared error (MSE) between the
empirical risk trajectory of SGD, denoted by <math id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}_{\text{SGD}}(k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mtext>SGD</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}_{\text{SGD}}(k)</annotation></semantics></math>, and
the theoretical prediction from FSL, <math id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>. Formally, we solve the
following optimization problem:</p>
<table id="A2.Ex26" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex26.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\min_{c_{1},c_{2},c_{3}}\frac{1}{K}\sum_{k=1}^{K}\left(\mathcal{E}_{\text{SGD}}(k)-\mathcal{E}_{k}\right)^{2}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mi>min</mi><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>K</mi></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>K</mi></munderover><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mtext>SGD</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\min_{c_{1},c_{2},c_{3}}\frac{1}{K}\sum_{k=1}^{K}\left(\mathcal{E}_{\text{SGD}}(k)-\mathcal{E}_{k}\right)^{2},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p3.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> represents the total number of training steps. This minimization is
performed using ordinary least squares (OLS), with the integrals in the FSL
expression <math id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> evaluated numerically via quadrature methods.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">We display the learning rate schedules (LRSs) used in the SGD experiments in the top-left panel of Figure <a href="#A2.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ Fitting FSL on SGD Average-Risks. ‣ B.2 Teacher-Student Kernel Regression ‣ Appendix B Experiment Details and Additional Results ‣ Appendix ‣ Unveiling the Role of Learning Rate Schedules via Functional Scaling Laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>. Complementing Figure <a href="#S4.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 4 Functional Scaling Laws ‣ Unveiling the Role of Learning Rate Schedules via Functional Scaling Laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> (middle and right), additional experimental results for various values of <math id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> are presented in Figure <a href="#A2.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ Fitting FSL on SGD Average-Risks. ‣ B.2 Teacher-Student Kernel Regression ‣ Appendix B Experiment Details and Additional Results ‣ Appendix ‣ Unveiling the Role of Learning Rate Schedules via Functional Scaling Laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A2.F4" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_3"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.19189v1/sgd_lrs.svg" id="A2.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:143/113;" width="143" height="113"></object></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_3"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.19189v1/fit_FSL_0.5_3_relative.svg" id="A2.F4.g2" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:143/115;" width="143" height="115"></object></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_3"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.19189v1/fit_FSL_0.5_5_relative.svg" id="A2.F4.g3" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:143/115;" width="143" height="115"></object></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.19189v1/fit_FSL_0.8_3_relative.svg" id="A2.F4.g4" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:143/115;" width="143" height="115"></object></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.19189v1/fit_FSL_0.8_5_relative.svg" id="A2.F4.g5" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:143/115;" width="143" height="115"></object></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A2.F4.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 4</span>: </span><span id="A2.F4.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Fitting results of FSL on SGD trajectories.<span id="A2.F4.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">
The shaded curves are the average over 200 independent
SGD runs, while the solid curves show the predictions of FSL.
</span></span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Scaling law experiments.</h6>

<div id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">These experiments are designed to evaluate the correctness of the scaling laws predicted by our analytical analysis.
To this end, we conduct two complementary sets of experiments:</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="A2.I2" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A2.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A2.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.I2.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">FSL experiments.</span> This experiment is intended to validate the theoretical predictions derived from the FSL. We compute the predicted risk by numerically discretizing the FSL (<a href="#S4.E7" title="In Theorem 4.1 (Intrinsic-time Functional Scaling Law (FSL)). ‣ 4 Functional Scaling Laws ‣ Unveiling the Role of Learning Rate Schedules via Functional Scaling Laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>), with all untracked constants set to 1.
For each LRS, following the theoretical analysis, we set <math id="A2.I2.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta_{\max}=0.05D^{-r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>0.05</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>D</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta_{\max}=0.05D^{-r}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="A2.I2.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=s/(1+s)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=s/(1+s)</annotation></semantics></math> for the constant learning rate schedule, and <math id="A2.I2.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=1</annotation></semantics></math> for exponential decay and WSD schedules.
We fix the batch size to <math id="A2.I2.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B=1</annotation></semantics></math>; thus, for each data budget <math id="A2.I2.i1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math>, we compute the intrinsic time and evaluate the final-step risk using the discretized FSL.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A2.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A2.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.I2.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">SGD experiments.</span> This experiment serves to assess whether the scaling behavior predicted by our continuous-time FSL faithfully captures that of discrete-time SGD.
We simulate stochastic gradient descent (SGD) with 200 independent trajectories and a fixed batch size <math id="A2.I2.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B=1</annotation></semantics></math>.
For each data budget <math id="A2.I2.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math>, the maximum learning rate is set as <math id="A2.I2.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta_{\max}=0.05D^{-r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>0.05</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>D</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta_{\max}=0.05D^{-r}</annotation></semantics></math>, using the same theoretical values of <math id="A2.I2.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> as in the FSL experiments.
We run SGD for <math id="A2.I2.i2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> steps under each corresponding LRS and record the final-step excess risk.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.3 </span>LLM pre-training</h4>

<section id="A2.SS3.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Practical FSL Ansatz for LLM pre-training</h6>

<div id="A2.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, in order to fit the scaling laws on real
LLM pre-training loss curves, we will derive an approximation form of the FSL in Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 4.1 (Intrinsic-time Functional Scaling Law (FSL)). ‣ 4 Functional Scaling Laws ‣ Unveiling the Role of Learning Rate Schedules via Functional Scaling Laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">First, by the physical-time for of the FSL (<a href="#S4.E7" title="In Theorem 4.1 (Intrinsic-time Functional Scaling Law (FSL)). ‣ 4 Functional Scaling Laws ‣ Unveiling the Role of Learning Rate Schedules via Functional Scaling Laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>) with <math id="A2.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h=1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B(u)\equiv B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≡</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B(u)\equiv B</annotation></semantics></math>, we have</p>
<table id="A2.Ex27" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex27.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{E}_{k}\eqsim\frac{1}{T(k)^{s}}+M^{-s\beta}+\frac{1}{B}\int_{0}^{k}\mathcal{K}(T(k)-T(u))(\sigma^{2}+e(T(u)))\cdot\varphi(u)^{2}\mathop{}\!\mathrm{d}u." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>≂</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>s</mi></msup></mrow></mfrac><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>M</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow></mrow></msup><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>B</mi></mfrac><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mn>0</mn><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>T</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>φ</mi><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mphantom></mphantom><mi>d</mi><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}_{k}\eqsim\frac{1}{T(k)^{s}}+M^{-s\beta}+\frac{1}{B}\int_{0}^{k}\mathcal{K}(T(k)-T(u))(\sigma^{2}+e(T(u)))\cdot\varphi(u)^{2}\mathop{}\!\mathrm{d}u.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">Here we focus on the integral term.
Since <math id="A2.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi(u)=\int_{0}^{u}\varphi^{\prime}(r)\mathop{}\!\mathrm{d}r+\varphi(0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mn>0</mn><mi>u</mi></msubsup><mrow><msup><mi>φ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi(u)=\int_{0}^{u}\varphi^{\prime}(r)\mathop{}\!\mathrm{d}r+\varphi(0)</annotation></semantics></math>, and that</p>
<table id="A2.E10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.E10.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\int_{0}^{k}\mathcal{K}(T(k)-T(u))(\sigma^{2}+e(T(k))\cdot\varphi(u)\varphi(0)\mathop{}\!\mathrm{d}u=\varphi(0)\int_{0}^{T(k)}\mathcal{K}(T(k)-t)(\sigma^{2}+e(t))\mathop{}\!\mathrm{d}t." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mn>0</mn><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>T</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>φ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>φ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mi>d</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>φ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mn>0</mn><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mi>d</mi><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\int_{0}^{k}\mathcal{K}(T(k)-T(u))(\sigma^{2}+e(T(k))\cdot\varphi(u)\varphi(0)\mathop{}\!\mathrm{d}u=\varphi(0)\int_{0}^{T(k)}\mathcal{K}(T(k)-t)(\sigma^{2}+e(t))\mathop{}\!\mathrm{d}t.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(10)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.3" class="ltx_p">Note that this is exactly the SGD noise term at the constant LRS
<math id="A2.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta(0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta(0)</annotation></semantics></math> for a total intrinsic-time <math id="A2.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T(\tau)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T(\tau)</annotation></semantics></math>.
By results of constant LRS (as seen in the proof of Theorem <a href="#A4.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem D.1 (Restatement of Theorem ). ‣ D.1 Proofs for Constant LRS ‣ Appendix D Proofs for Section ‣ Appendix ‣ Unveiling the Role of Learning Rate Schedules via Functional Scaling Laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D.1</span></a>), we have</p>
<table id="A2.Ex28" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex28.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eqref{eq:numerical_const_term}\eqsim\varphi(0)(\sigma^{2}+e(T(k)))." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>(</mtext><mtext><a href="#A2.E10" title="In Practical FSL Ansatz for LLM pre-training ‣ B.3 LLM pre-training ‣ Appendix B Experiment Details and Additional Results ‣ Appendix ‣ Unveiling the Role of Learning Rate Schedules via Functional Scaling Laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a></mtext><mtext>)</mtext></mrow><mo>≂</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eqref{eq:numerical_const_term}\eqsim\varphi(0)(\sigma^{2}+e(T(k))).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">As <math id="A2.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi(0)\lesssim 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≲</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi(0)\lesssim 1</annotation></semantics></math>, we have</p>
<table id="A2.E11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.E11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}_{k}\eqsim\frac{1}{T(k)^{s}}+M^{-s\beta}-\mathrm{LRD}(k)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>≂</mo><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>s</mi></msup></mrow></mfrac><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>M</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>LRD</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}_{k}\eqsim\frac{1}{T(k)^{s}}+M^{-s\beta}-\mathrm{LRD}(k),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(11)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p3.2" class="ltx_p">where</p>
<table id="A2.EGx4" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A2.Ex29"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex29.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathrm{LRD}(k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>LRD</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathrm{LRD}(k)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex29.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle:=-\frac{1}{B}\int_{0}^{k}\mathcal{K}(T(k)-T(u))(\sigma^{2}+e(T(u)))\varphi(u)\int_{0}^{u}\varphi^{\prime}(r)\mathop{}\!\mathrm{d}r\mathop{}\!\mathrm{d}u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0em">:</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>B</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mstyle displaystyle="true"><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mn>0</mn><mi>k</mi></msubsup></mstyle><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>T</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>φ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mn>0</mn><mi>u</mi></msubsup></mstyle><msup><mi>φ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mi>d</mi><mi>r</mi><mphantom></mphantom><mi>d</mi><mi>u</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle:=-\frac{1}{B}\int_{0}^{k}\mathcal{K}(T(k)-T(u))(\sigma^{2}+e(T(u)))\varphi(u)\int_{0}^{u}\varphi^{\prime}(r)\mathop{}\!\mathrm{d}r\mathop{}\!\mathrm{d}u</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A2.Ex30"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex30.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle=-\frac{1}{B}\int_{0}^{k}\varphi^{\prime}(r)\int_{r}^{k}\mathcal{K}(T(k)-T(u))(\sigma^{2}+e(T(u)))\varphi(u)\mathop{}\!\mathrm{d}u\mathop{}\!\mathrm{d}r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>B</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mstyle displaystyle="true"><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mn>0</mn><mi>k</mi></msubsup></mstyle><msup><mi>φ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mi>r</mi><mi>k</mi></msubsup></mstyle><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>T</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>φ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mi>d</mi><mi>u</mi><mphantom></mphantom><mi>d</mi><mi>r</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=-\frac{1}{B}\int_{0}^{k}\varphi^{\prime}(r)\int_{r}^{k}\mathcal{K}(T(k)-T(u))(\sigma^{2}+e(T(u)))\varphi(u)\mathop{}\!\mathrm{d}u\mathop{}\!\mathrm{d}r</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A2.Ex31"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex31.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle=-\frac{1}{B}\int_{0}^{k}\varphi^{\prime}(r)\int_{T(r)}^{T(k)}\mathcal{K}(T(k)-t)(\sigma^{2}+e(t))\mathop{}\!\mathrm{d}t\mathop{}\!\mathrm{d}r." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>B</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mstyle displaystyle="true"><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mn>0</mn><mi>k</mi></msubsup></mstyle><msup><mi>φ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msubsup></mstyle><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mi>d</mi><mi>t</mi><mphantom></mphantom><mi>d</mi><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=-\frac{1}{B}\int_{0}^{k}\varphi^{\prime}(r)\int_{T(r)}^{T(k)}\mathcal{K}(T(k)-t)(\sigma^{2}+e(t))\mathop{}\!\mathrm{d}t\mathop{}\!\mathrm{d}r.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">We discretize the outer integral at integer nodes <math id="A2.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=0,1,\dots,k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=0,1,\dots,k</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
<table id="A2.Ex32" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex32.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{LRD}(k)\approx\frac{1}{B}\sum_{i=1}^{k}(\eta_{i-1}-\eta_{i})\int_{T(i)}^{T(k)}\mathcal{K}(T(k)-t)(\sigma^{2}+e(t))\mathop{}\!\mathrm{d}t." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>LRD</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≈</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>B</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></munderover><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msubsup><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{LRD}(k)\approx\frac{1}{B}\sum_{i=1}^{k}(\eta_{i-1}-\eta_{i})\int_{T(i)}^{T(k)}\mathcal{K}(T(k)-t)(\sigma^{2}+e(t))\mathop{}\!\mathrm{d}t.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p4.2" class="ltx_p">By the integral mean value theorem, we can take <math id="A2.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\sigma^{2}+e(t))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\sigma^{2}+e(t))</annotation></semantics></math>
outside the integral, which gives</p>
<table id="A2.Ex33" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex33.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{LRD}(k)\approx\frac{1}{B}\sum_{i=1}^{k}(\eta_{i-1}-\eta_{i})(\sigma^{2}+e(\xi_{i}))\int_{T(i)}^{T(k)}\mathcal{K}(T(k)-t)\mathop{}\!\mathrm{d}t," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>LRD</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≈</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>B</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></munderover><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msubsup><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{LRD}(k)\approx\frac{1}{B}\sum_{i=1}^{k}(\eta_{i-1}-\eta_{i})(\sigma^{2}+e(\xi_{i}))\int_{T(i)}^{T(k)}\mathcal{K}(T(k)-t)\mathop{}\!\mathrm{d}t,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p4.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A2.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi_{i}\in[T(i),T(k)]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi_{i}\in[T(i),T(k)]</annotation></semantics></math>. Now since</p>
<table id="A2.Ex34" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex34.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\int_{T(i)}^{T(k)}\mathcal{K}(T(k)-t)\mathop{}\!\mathrm{d}t=\int_{M^{-\beta}}^{1}u^{1-1/\beta}\int_{T(i)}^{T(k)}e^{-2u(T(k)-t)}\mathop{}\!\mathrm{d}t\mathop{}\!\mathrm{d}u=\frac{1}{2}-\frac{1}{2}g_{1-1/\beta}(T(k)-T(i))," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msubsup><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><msup><mi>M</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow></msup><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mrow><msup><mi>u</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msubsup><mrow><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>−</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\int_{T(i)}^{T(k)}\mathcal{K}(T(k)-t)\mathop{}\!\mathrm{d}t=\int_{M^{-\beta}}^{1}u^{1-1/\beta}\int_{T(i)}^{T(k)}e^{-2u(T(k)-t)}\mathop{}\!\mathrm{d}t\mathop{}\!\mathrm{d}u=\frac{1}{2}-\frac{1}{2}g_{1-1/\beta}(T(k)-T(i)),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p4.4" class="ltx_p">we then further simplify it as (see the definition of <math id="A2.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> function in Section <a href="#A3.SS3" title="C.3 The g Functions ‣ Appendix C Proof of Theorem ‣ Appendix ‣ Unveiling the Role of Learning Rate Schedules via Functional Scaling Laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C.3</span></a>)</p>
<table id="A2.EGx5" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A2.Ex35"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex35.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathrm{LRD}(k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>LRD</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathrm{LRD}(k)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex35.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\approx\frac{1}{2B}\sum_{i=1}^{k}(\eta_{i-1}-\eta_{i})(\sigma^{2}+e(\xi_{i}))(1-g_{1-1/\beta}(T(k)-T(i)))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≈</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\approx\frac{1}{2B}\sum_{i=1}^{k}(\eta_{i-1}-\eta_{i})(\sigma^{2}+e(\xi_{i}))(1-g_{1-1/\beta}(T(k)-T(i)))</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A2.Ex36"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex36.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\approx\frac{1}{2B}\sum_{i=1}^{k}(\eta_{i-1}-\eta_{i})(\sigma^{2}+e(T(i)))(1-(1+c(T(k)-T(i)))^{-\gamma})." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≈</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\approx\frac{1}{2B}\sum_{i=1}^{k}(\eta_{i-1}-\eta_{i})(\sigma^{2}+e(T(i)))(1-(1+c(T(k)-T(i)))^{-\gamma}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p4.5" class="ltx_p">Here, in the last line we approximate <math id="A2.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="A2.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T(i)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T(i)</annotation></semantics></math>
and approximate <math id="A2.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{1-1/\beta}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{1-1/\beta}(t)</annotation></semantics></math> as
<math id="A2.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1+ct)^{-\gamma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1+ct)^{-\gamma}</annotation></semantics></math> with some constant <math id="A2.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> for simplicity.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p5.2" class="ltx_p">Therefore, combining with (<a href="#A2.E11" title="In Practical FSL Ansatz for LLM pre-training ‣ B.3 LLM pre-training ‣ Appendix B Experiment Details and Additional Results ‣ Appendix ‣ Unveiling the Role of Learning Rate Schedules via Functional Scaling Laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a>),
when the batch size <math id="A2.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> is fixed, after renaming some constants,
the final discrete ansatz can be written as</p>
<table id="A2.E12" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="A2.E12X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.E12X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{R}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{R}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.E12X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\approx c_{0}+\frac{c_{1}}{T(k)^{s}}+c_{2}M^{-s\beta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>s</mi></msup></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>M</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\approx c_{0}+\frac{c_{1}}{T(k)^{s}}+c_{2}M^{-s\beta}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="2" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(12)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="A2.E12Xa" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.E12Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle-c_{3}\sum_{i=1}^{k}(\eta_{i-1}-\eta_{i})\left(c_{4}+\frac{1}{T(i)^{s}}\right)\left(1-(1+c_{5}(T(k)-T(i)))^{-\gamma}\right)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>c</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>3</mn></msub><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo></mstyle><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>k</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>η</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>η</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>(</mo><mi>c</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>4</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>s</mi></msup></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo><mo>(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>c</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>5</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow></msup><mo>)</mo><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle-c_{3}\sum_{i=1}^{k}(\eta_{i-1}-\eta_{i})\left(c_{4}+\frac{1}{T(i)^{s}}\right)\left(1-(1+c_{5}(T(k)-T(i)))^{-\gamma}\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p5.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A2.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A2.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A2.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A2.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{3}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A2.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{4}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A2.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{5}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>5</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{5}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A2.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A2.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A2.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math> are
constants to fit.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS3.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Fitting the Practical FSL</h6>

<div id="A2.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The objective of this experiment is to analyze and fit the loss function using
our functional scaling law, by (<a href="#A2.E12" title="In Practical FSL Ansatz for LLM pre-training ‣ B.3 LLM pre-training ‣ Appendix B Experiment Details and Additional Results ‣ Appendix ‣ Unveiling the Role of Learning Rate Schedules via Functional Scaling Laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a>),</p>
<table id="A2.Ex37" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex37.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\Theta}(k)=L_{0}+\frac{c_{1}}{T(k)^{s}}+c_{2}M^{-s\beta}-\operatorname{LRD}(k)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>s</mi></msup></mrow></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>M</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>LRD</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\Theta}(k)=L_{0}+\frac{c_{1}}{T(k)^{s}}+c_{2}M^{-s\beta}-\operatorname{LRD}(k)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where</p>
<table id="A2.Ex38" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex38.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{LRD}(k):=c_{3}\sum_{i=1}^{k}(\eta_{i-1}-\eta_{i})\left(c_{4}+\frac{1}{T(i)^{s}}\right)\left(1-\frac{1}{(1+c_{5}(T(k)-T(i)))^{\gamma}}\right)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>LRD</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></munderover><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>s</mi></msup></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>5</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>γ</mi></msup></mfrac></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{LRD}(k):=c_{3}\sum_{i=1}^{k}(\eta_{i-1}-\eta_{i})\left(c_{4}+\frac{1}{T(i)^{s}}\right)\left(1-\frac{1}{(1+c_{5}(T(k)-T(i)))^{\gamma}}\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.3" class="ltx_p">and <math id="A2.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Theta=(L_{0},c_{1},c_{2},c_{3},c_{4},c_{5},s,\beta,\gamma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>5</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Theta=(L_{0},c_{1},c_{2},c_{3},c_{4},c_{5},s,\beta,\gamma)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Following <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib60" title="" class="ltx_ref">60</a>]</cite>, we utilize the Huber loss as the objective function.</p>
<table id="A2.Ex39" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex39.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\min_{\Theta}\sum_{k=1}^{K}\text{Huber}_{\delta}\left(\log\mathcal{L}_{\Theta}(k)-\log\mathcal{L}_{\text{gt}}(k)\right)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mrow><mmultiscripts><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>K</mi></munderover><mprescripts></mprescripts><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts><mo>⁡</mo><msub><mtext>Huber</mtext><mi>δ</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>gt</mtext></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\min_{\Theta}\sum_{k=1}^{K}\text{Huber}_{\delta}\left(\log\mathcal{L}_{\Theta}(k)-\log\mathcal{L}_{\text{gt}}(k)\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A2.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta=1\times 10^{-3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta=1\times 10^{-3}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A2.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{gt}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>gt</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{gt}}</annotation></semantics></math>
denotes the ground truth of the validation
losses.
We adopt the Adam optimizer, with a learning rate of <math id="A2.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5\times 10^{-2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5\times 10^{-2}</annotation></semantics></math> for the
index parameters in our law and <math id="A2.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5\times 10^{-3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5\times 10^{-3}</annotation></semantics></math> for the coefficient or
constant parameters. Each optimization takes over 10,000 steps.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We fit the law on the 400M model and 1B model trained with 20B tokens and an 8-1-1
LRS
We then predict the loss curve for the 400M model and 1B model with cosine LRS
and WSD LRS.
The experiment result is present in Figure <a href="#S6.F3.sf1" title="In Figure 3 ‣ FSL can fit and predict loss curves. ‣ 6.2 LLM Pre-training ‣ 6 Experiments ‣ Unveiling the Role of Learning Rate Schedules via Functional Scaling Laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3(a)</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS3.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">FSL-optimal LRS via numerical variation.</h6>

<div id="A2.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We propose to obtain a numerical optimal LRS by directly minimizing the final-step risk over the space of LRS using the fitted FSL, termed FSL-optimal LRS.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Step 1: Fitting FSL.</span>
Fit FSL on the loss
curve of a 1B QwenMoE model trained on 20B tokens with batch size 288, maximum learning rate
<math id="A2.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta_{0}=0.001" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.001</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta_{0}=0.001</annotation></semantics></math>, and the 8-1-1 scheduler over a total step of <math id="A2.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K=33907" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>33907</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K=33907</annotation></semantics></math>
, following the same procedure described earlier.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Step 2: Optimize LRS.</span>
To improve optimization stability, we reparameterize the learning rate schedule by defining</p>
<table id="A2.Ex40" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex40.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\delta_{i}=\eta_{i}-\eta_{i+1},\text{ for }i=0,1,\dots,K-1." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>η</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>η</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mtext> for </mtext><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{i}=\eta_{i}-\eta_{i+1},\text{ for }i=0,1,\dots,K-1.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p3.2" class="ltx_p">Then, the <math id="A2.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-th step learning rate can be recovered by <math id="A2.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta_{i}=\eta_{0}-\sum_{k=0}^{i-1}\delta_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.055em">−</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta_{i}=\eta_{0}-\sum_{k=0}^{i-1}\delta_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>, which defines a one-to-one
correspondence between the learning rate schedule <math id="A2.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\eta_{i}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\eta_{i}\}</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="A2.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\delta_{i}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\delta_{i}\}</annotation></semantics></math>. The optimization problem is</p>
<table id="A2.E13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.E13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\min_{\{\delta_{i}\}_{i=1}^{K}}\mathcal{L}_{\Theta}(\{\eta_{i}\}_{i=1}^{K}),\quad\mathop{\hbox{subject to}}\,\sum_{k=0}^{K-1}\delta_{k}\leqslant\eta_{0},\,d\eta_{i}\geqslant 0,i=0,1,\dots,K-1." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mi>min</mi><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>K</mi></msubsup></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>K</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mtext>subject to</mtext><mo lspace="0.337em">⁡</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>⩽</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>⩾</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\min_{\{\delta_{i}\}_{i=1}^{K}}\mathcal{L}_{\Theta}(\{\eta_{i}\}_{i=1}^{K}),\quad\mathop{\hbox{subject to}}\,\sum_{k=0}^{K-1}\delta_{k}\leqslant\eta_{0},\,d\eta_{i}\geqslant 0,i=0,1,\dots,K-1.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(13)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p3.3" class="ltx_p">To solve the above constraint optimization, we use the projected gradient descent (PGD) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite>. The learning rate of PGD is searched
ranging from <math id="A2.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\times 10^{-8}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\times 10^{-8}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="A2.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5\times 10^{-10}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5\times 10^{-10}</annotation></semantics></math>,
and the optimization step number ranges from 50,000 to 100,000.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The resulting FSL-optimal LRS is presented in Figure <a href="#S6.F3.sf2" title="In Figure 3 ‣ FSL can fit and predict loss curves. ‣ 6.2 LLM Pre-training ‣ 6 Experiments ‣ Unveiling the Role of Learning Rate Schedules via Functional Scaling Laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3(b)</span></a> (left), where cosine, WSD, and 8-1-1 LRSs are also given for a comparison.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Step 3: Evaluate our LRS.</span> We then evaluate the performance of the resulting FSL-optimal LRS, and the three LRSs in Figure <a href="#S6.F3.sf2" title="In Figure 3 ‣ FSL can fit and predict loss curves. ‣ 6.2 LLM Pre-training ‣ 6 Experiments ‣ Unveiling the Role of Learning Rate Schedules via Functional Scaling Laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3(b)</span></a> (left) are used as baseline.
All comparisons are conducted on the same 1B QwenMoE model under identical training conditions: 33,907 total steps, batch size 288, and 20B training tokens. Full loss curves are shown in Figure <a href="#S6.F3.sf2" title="In Figure 3 ‣ FSL can fit and predict loss curves. ‣ 6.2 LLM Pre-training ‣ 6 Experiments ‣ Unveiling the Role of Learning Rate Schedules via Functional Scaling Laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3(b)</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS3.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Additional Experiments</h6>

<div id="A2.SS3.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS3.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We have further conducted ablation experiments with different model sizes and architectures, different total steps and different WSD schedules.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS3.SSS0.Px4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS3.SSS0.Px4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We validate our functional scaling law in models with various sizes, ranging from 100M to 1B, and diverse architectures including GPT-2 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">54</a>]</cite>, LLaMA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib61" title="" class="ltx_ref">61</a>]</cite> and QwenMoE <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib69" title="" class="ltx_ref">69</a>]</cite>.
For each model, we first fit the FSL using the 8-1-1 LRS and subsequently employ it to predict the loss curve under a WSD LRS.
Next we numerically solve the FSL-optimal LRS and empirically validate its efficacy by comparing the final pre-training loss against those obtained using other commonly adopted learning rate schedules.
We present the results in Figure <a href="#A2.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ Additional Experiments ‣ B.3 LLM pre-training ‣ Appendix B Experiment Details and Additional Results ‣ Appendix ‣ Unveiling the Role of Learning Rate Schedules via Functional Scaling Laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> for the 1B LLaMA dense model, Figure <a href="#A2.F6" title="Figure 6 ‣ Additional Experiments ‣ B.3 LLM pre-training ‣ Appendix B Experiment Details and Additional Results ‣ Appendix ‣ Unveiling the Role of Learning Rate Schedules via Functional Scaling Laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> for the 100M GPT-2 dense model.
The consistent alignment between predicted and observed performance across architectures and sizes underscores the robustness and generalizability FSL.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS3.SSS0.Px4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS3.SSS0.Px4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We further validate the applicability of our functional scaling law (FSL) across varying training durations. Using a 100M LLaMA dense model, we conduct experiments with total training steps set to 17k, 34k, 68k, and 134k. As demonstrated in Figures <a href="#A2.F7" title="Figure 7 ‣ Additional Experiments ‣ B.3 LLM pre-training ‣ Appendix B Experiment Details and Additional Results ‣ Appendix ‣ Unveiling the Role of Learning Rate Schedules via Functional Scaling Laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a> and <a href="#A2.F8" title="Figure 8 ‣ Additional Experiments ‣ B.3 LLM pre-training ‣ Appendix B Experiment Details and Additional Results ‣ Appendix ‣ Unveiling the Role of Learning Rate Schedules via Functional Scaling Laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>, our FSL accurately models the loss trajectories across all evaluated step counts, confirming its robustness to different total training steps.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS3.SSS0.Px4.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS3.SSS0.Px4.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Finally, we conduct a comprehensive empirical comparison between our FSL-optimal learning rate schedule and various WSD baselines, examining different decay ratios and minimum learning rate configurations. As evidenced by Figures <a href="#A2.F9" title="Figure 9 ‣ Additional Experiments ‣ B.3 LLM pre-training ‣ Appendix B Experiment Details and Additional Results ‣ Appendix ‣ Unveiling the Role of Learning Rate Schedules via Functional Scaling Laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a> and <a href="#A2.F10" title="Figure 10 ‣ Additional Experiments ‣ B.3 LLM pre-training ‣ Appendix B Experiment Details and Additional Results ‣ Appendix ‣ Unveiling the Role of Learning Rate Schedules via Functional Scaling Laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>, our FSL-optimal LRS consistently outperforms all WSD variants, achieving superior final pre-training loss across all experimental conditions. This systematic evaluation demonstrates both the effectiveness of our theoretically-derived schedule and its practical advantages over conventional heuristic approaches.</p>
</div>
